"""A Level Literature in English (0735), Lower Sixth: Literary Theory & Practical Criticism (4 lessons) and Shakespearean Drama (4 lessons).

Sources: KKEVO's master curriculum blueprint (modules L6M1 and L6M2) and the Board's 2023 Advanced Level subject report for Literature in English.
The report names Hamlet (old syllabus) and Coriolanus (new syllabus) as the Shakespeare texts of 2023; the blueprint says only 'two prescribed
Shakespearean plays', so every lesson tells students to check with their teacher which two plays their school teaches. Plot, character and theme
facts are given only for Hamlet, Coriolanus and a few universally taught plays (Macbeth, As You Like It, Twelfth Night, Henry V), in outline and in
our own words; students must check details against their own copy. All poems, extracts and passages written for the lessons are original and
invented. Theorists are named only where the attribution is standard; points on which textbooks differ are marked 'check your textbook'.
"""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = ("literature-in-english", "lower-sixth")

M_THEORY = "Literary Theory & Practical Criticism"
M_SHAKE = "Shakespearean Drama"

OWNCOPY = ("**Use your own copy and ask your teacher.** The Board changes its set texts from syllabus to syllabus, and this lesson gives you "
           "method and a few well-known facts in our own words. Check every fact against your own copy before you use it in an essay.")

LESSONS = []

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1. Aristotle
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_THEORY, topic="Aristotle's Poetics and tragedy",
    title="Aristotle's Poetics: what makes a tragedy",
    slug="lil1-aristotle-poetics",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Aristotle's Poetics defines tragedy as the imitation of a serious, complete action; this lesson explains its six parts, plot, hamartia, peripeteia, anagnorisis and catharsis, and how to use them (and question them) in an essay.",
    objectives=[
        "Define tragedy as Aristotle does and name its six parts, saying why he ranks plot first",
        "Explain hamartia, peripeteia, anagnorisis and catharsis, and say where scholars disagree",
        "Describe the tragic hero and the shape of a tragic plot",
        "Apply Aristotle's terms to a play, with evidence, and judge where they fit badly",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Poetics", "Tragedy", "Plot (mythos)", "Hamartia", "Peripeteia", "Anagnorisis", "Catharsis", "Tragic hero"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Why does a story about a king's downfall hold an audience in silence? A traditional storyteller in a village square knows that a great person who falls makes listeners feel fear and pity at once. More than two thousand years ago, the Greek philosopher **Aristotle** (4th century BC) tried to explain this effect in a short work called the **Poetics**. Most of what survives is about tragedy and epic poetry; the part on comedy is lost."),
        T("Almost every course on drama uses Aristotle's words: *hamartia, peripeteia, anagnorisis, catharsis*. The Board's report says A Level tasks reward candidates who can use ideas like these to judge a text, not only to name them. So you must know the terms and also their limits."),
        T(OWNCOPY),
        H("Aristotle's definition and six parts"),
        D("Aristotle describes **tragedy** as the imitation (representation) of a serious action that is complete and of a certain size, in language made pleasing, performed by actors and not told as narrative, and which arouses pity and fear so as to bring about a catharsis of such feelings. (Our paraphrase; translations differ.)", title="Tragedy in the Poetics"),
        T("He divides every tragedy into six parts, in order of importance: **plot** (the arrangement of events), **character** (the moral choices people make), **thought** (the reasoning and ideas in the speeches), **diction** (the language), **melody** (song and music of the chorus) and **spectacle** (the visual side: costume and staging)."),
        KEY("Why plot comes first", "Aristotle says tragedy is an imitation of action, not of people. A play can work without strong characterisation, but not without a well-built chain of events. Character matters because it shows in what people choose to do.", cards=[
            ["Plot", "The events arranged with cause and effect: one thing leads to the next. Aristotle wants a beginning, a middle and an end."],
            ["Unity of action", "One main action, with no scenes that could be removed without changing the whole. (The so-called three unities of time and place were added by later critics; Aristotle stresses action.)"],
        ]),
        IMG("allil1-tragic-arc.svg", alt="A rising and falling line for a tragic plot with labels: complication, peripeteia and anagnorisis at the turning point, catastrophe, catharsis.", caption="Figure 1. The shape of a tragic plot as Aristotle describes it: a rise, a turning point, a fall."),
        H("The four key terms"),
        D("**Hamartia** is the mistake or error that starts the hero's fall. Many students call it a 'tragic flaw'. In the Poetics it is closer to an error of judgement: a wrong act done through ignorance or misjudgement, and not wickedness. (Check your textbook: scholars argue about how far it means a character fault.)", title="Hamartia"),
        D("**Peripeteia** is a reversal: the action turns into its opposite, as when a messenger who comes to bring good news ends by bringing the opposite.", title="Peripeteia"),
        D("**Anagnorisis** is recognition: a change from ignorance to knowledge, often of who someone is or what one has done. Aristotle says the best plots have peripeteia and anagnorisis happen together.", title="Anagnorisis"),
        D("**Catharsis** is the effect on the audience of pity and fear. The word is usually translated purgation (a cleansing) or purification; some scholars say it means clarification. (Check your textbook: this is one of the most argued words in criticism.)", title="Catharsis"),
        H("The tragic hero"),
        T("Aristotle says the best tragic hero is neither perfectly good nor completely wicked. A very good person falling would shock us without being fair; a villain falling would please us. We pity someone who suffers undeservedly and we fear for someone **like ourselves**. So the hero is a person of high reputation and good fortune who falls because of a mistake (hamartia)."),
        T("Aristotle's favourite example is Sophocles' *Oedipus the King*, where the discovery of Oedipus' true identity is both a reversal and a recognition. For Shakespeare, remember that Aristotle never saw an Elizabethan play. His ideas help, but they are not rules that Shakespeare was following."),
        EX("Worked example 1: applying the terms", intro="Use Aristotle's terms on this invented plot. Adaeze, a respected village chief, has banished a young man for stealing yams. Later she learns that he was innocent and was framed by her own nephew. Before she can undo the harm, the young man has died in exile.", steps=[
            S("Hamartia", text="Her error of judgement is acting on a report without checking the facts. She is not wicked: she believes she is doing justice."),
            S("Peripeteia", text="Her act of justice turns into injustice and then into a death. Her good intention produces the opposite result."),
            S("Anagnorisis", text="She recognises that the young man was innocent and that her nephew lied. This change from ignorance to knowledge is the recognition."),
            S("Catharsis", text="The audience feels pity for the young man and for Adaeze, and fear that a good ruler can be wrong in this way. The feelings are released as the play closes."),
        ]),
        H("Using Aristotle well, and questioning him"),
        T("Aristotle writes about Greek tragedy. When you apply him to a play such as *Hamlet* or *Coriolanus*, say what fits and what does not. A good answer uses the terms as **tools of argument**: 'Coriolanus' reversal comes when ... and this fits Aristotle because ...'."),
        EX("Worked example 2: testing a term against a play", intro="Question (written for practice): 'Is Hamlet's delay a hamartia in Aristotle's sense?' Plan the argument.", steps=[
            S("State the term", text="Hamartia is an error of judgement that begins the fall, not necessarily a permanent flaw."),
            S("Argue for", text="Hamlet's long delay in avenging his father, and his decision to test the ghost's claim first, lead to the deaths of several innocent people, including Polonius and Ophelia."),
            S("Argue against", text="Critics argue that the delay is caused by moral doubt and by the difficulty of the task, not an error. Other characters' actions (Claudius' plots) also drive the fall."),
            S("Judge", text="The best answer says Aristotle's term fits partly: Hamlet makes serious mistakes, but the play shows a hero caught in a corrupt world, so 'hamartia' is a useful but incomplete label."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Calling hamartia 'the tragic flaw' and nothing else", "The phrase is popular, but the Poetics speaks of an error. Show you know both meanings: say 'a mistake or flaw in judgement'."),
        MISTAKE("Naming terms without evidence", "A paragraph that says 'there is peripeteia and anagnorisis' without a scene or an event earns little. Name the moment, then explain what changes."),
        QUIZ("According to the Poetics, which part of tragedy is the most important?", ["Spectacle", "Plot", "Diction", "Melody"], 1,
             "Aristotle places plot first because tragedy imitates action.", hint="Which part is about what happens?",
             feedback=["Spectacle is the least important for Aristotle: it depends on the staging, not the poet's art.", "", "Diction is third in his list of importance, after plot and character.", "Melody is a pleasing addition, ranked fifth."]),
        QUIZ("A hero suddenly learns that the man he has killed was his own brother. Which term names this change from ignorance to knowledge?", ["Peripeteia", "Hamartia", "Anagnorisis", "Catharsis"], 2,
             "Anagnorisis is recognition: a change from ignorance to knowledge.", hint="Look for the word meaning recognition.",
             feedback=["Peripeteia is a reversal of fortune, not a discovery.", "Hamartia is the error that starts the fall.", "", "Catharsis is the emotional release of the audience."]),
        PQ("(a) Define 'tragic hero' as Aristotle describes him. (b) Explain two limits of applying Aristotle's ideas to a play by Shakespeare.",
           "(a) A person of high standing and good fortune who is neither wholly good nor wholly bad, and who falls because of a hamartia (an error of judgement), so that the audience feels pity and fear. (b) Any two: Aristotle was describing Greek tragedy and did not know Shakespeare's theatre; Shakespeare's plays mix comic and serious scenes and have many subplots, while Aristotle prefers a single tight action; Shakespeare often shows social and political forces behind the fall, not just one error; the meanings of hamartia and catharsis are disputed, so they cannot be used as exact rules."),
        PQ("Write a plan (not a full essay) for: 'Discuss how far peripeteia and anagnorisis drive the plot of a tragedy you have studied.'",
           "Introduction: define both terms and say whether they occur together. Point 1: the turning point of the play (name the scene) and what is reversed (fortune, plan or relationship). Point 2: the moment of recognition (who learns what, and with what words or actions). Point 3: how the discovery or reversal causes the catastrophe. Evaluation: the extent to which these terms fit (for example, a play with a gradual fall has no single sharp reversal). Conclusion: a judgement answering 'how far'."),
        PQ("In four sentences, explain why Aristotle thinks plot matters more than character in tragedy.",
           "Aristotle says tragedy imitates action and life, and happiness or misery lie in action. Characters are shown through what they choose to do, so character depends on plot. A tragedy with weak characterisation can still move the audience if the events are well arranged, but a play of fine speeches without a strong plot cannot. Plot also produces the reversals and recognitions that cause pity and fear."),
        SUM("Aristotle's Poetics defines tragedy as the imitation of a serious, complete action that produces pity and fear and so a catharsis.",
            "It has six parts: plot, character, thought, diction, melody and spectacle, with plot the most important.",
            "Hamartia is an error of judgement (not only a flaw), peripeteia is reversal, anagnorisis is recognition, and catharsis is the emotional release (its exact meaning is disputed).",
            "The tragic hero is neither wholly good nor wholly bad and is like us; the fall comes through a mistake.",
            "Use the terms with evidence from the text, and say where they fit badly."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2. Prosody and scansion
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_THEORY, topic="Prosody and scansion",
    title="Prosody: metre, scansion and stanza forms",
    slug="lil1-prosody-scansion",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Scansion means marking stressed and unstressed syllables, grouping them into feet (iamb, trochee, anapaest, dactyl), naming the line length, and describing stanza and rhyme scheme and their effect.",
    objectives=[
        "Mark stressed and unstressed syllables and divide a line into feet",
        "Name the four main feet and the common line lengths, for example iambic pentameter",
        "Identify rhyme schemes and stanza forms (couplet, quatrain, sonnet, ballad stanza, blank verse)",
        "Comment on the effect of metre, enjambment and caesura instead of only naming them",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Prosody", "Scansion", "Foot", "Iamb", "Trochee", "Anapaest", "Dactyl", "Pentameter", "Rhyme scheme", "Blank verse", "Caesura", "Enjambment"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Clap the rhythm of a well-known song or a church hymn and you feel it: strong beats and weak beats. A poem has such a beat too, and **prosody** is the study of it. The Board's report says that scansion (marking the rhythm of a poem) remains a problem in the unseen poetry question: many candidates could state a rhyme scheme but could not say why it was effective. This lesson gives you the method."),
        T("All poems used here are written for this lesson. Stress marks use capital letters for stressed syllables."),
        H("Stress, syllables and feet"),
        D("**Prosody** is the study of the sound patterns of verse: metre, rhythm, rhyme and stanza form. **Scansion** is the marking of a line to show its pattern of stressed (strong) and unstressed (weak) syllables.", title="Prosody and scansion"),
        D("A **foot** is the unit of metre: a small group of stressed and unstressed syllables repeated through a line. We mark an unstressed syllable with u and a stressed syllable with /.", title="Foot"),
        KEY("The four main feet", "Learn these four. Each pattern is shown with an example word or phrase written in our own words.", cards=[
            ["Iamb (u /)", "Weak then strong: 'be-GIN', 'the RAIN'. The commonest English foot. Sounds natural, like speech."],
            ["Trochee (/ u)", "Strong then weak: 'HUR-ry', 'DOWN the'. Sounds firm, urgent or chant-like."],
            ["Anapaest (u u /)", "Two weak then strong: 'at the END'. Sounds quick and galloping."],
            ["Dactyl (/ u u)", "Strong then two weak: 'SLOW-ly the'. Sounds flowing, like a slow roll."],
        ]),
        T("Two lesser feet also appear. A **spondee** has two stressed syllables (/ /) and slows the line. A **pyrrhic** has two unstressed syllables (u u) and speeds it up. They are used as variations inside lines of another metre."),
        H("Line length"),
        D("The number of feet in a line names its length: **monometer** (1), **dimeter** (2), **trimeter** (3), **tetrameter** (4), **pentameter** (5), **hexameter** (6). We name a line by foot and length: an iamb repeated five times is **iambic pentameter**, ten syllables in all.", title="Naming a line"),
        IMG("allil1-feet-patterns.svg", alt="Four lines of verse shown with stress marks under each syllable: iambic pentameter, trochaic tetrameter, anapaestic tetrameter, and a dactylic line.", caption="Figure 1. The four main feet in lines written for this lesson. Capitals mark stressed syllables."),
        EX("Worked example 1: scanning a line", intro="Scan this line (written for practice) and name its metre:  the rain has stopped the market stalls begin", steps=[
            S("Split into syllables", text="the / rain / has / stopped / the / mar / ket / stalls / be / gin: ten syllables."),
            S("Mark the stress", text="the RAIN has STOPPED the MAR-ket STALLS be-GIN. The stresses fall on rain, stopped, mar-, stalls, -gin."),
            S("Group into feet", text="(the RAIN) (has STOPPED) (the MAR) (ket STALLS) (be GIN): five weak-strong pairs."),
            S("Name it", text="Five iambs: iambic pentameter. The pattern is steady, like calm speech, which suits a quiet scene."),
        ], check=r"10 \text{ syllables} = 5 \text{ feet} \times 2"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a line with a different metre", intro="Scan: DOWN the ROAD the TRA-ders HUR-ry", steps=[
            S("Count syllables", text="down / the / road / the / tra / ders / hur / ry: eight syllables."),
            S("Group into feet", text="(DOWN the) (ROAD the) (TRA ders) (HUR ry): each foot is strong then weak."),
            S("Name it", text="Four trochees: trochaic tetrameter. The strong start of each foot gives a brisk, marching feel that matches the hurrying traders."),
        ]),
        H("Stanza forms and rhyme"),
        T("A **stanza** is a group of lines in a poem, like a paragraph in prose. We show rhyme scheme with letters: lines that rhyme get the same letter. A poem whose end sounds are *day, sea, way, tree* has the scheme **abab** if the lines run day-sea-way-tree."),
        KEY("Common forms to know", "Know the pattern of each form and the usual effect.", cards=[
            ["Couplet", "Two rhyming lines (aa). Gives a neat, closing or witty feel."],
            ["Quatrain", "Four lines. Common schemes: abab, abba, aabb. The **ballad stanza** is a quatrain, usually alternating four and three beats, often rhyming abcb."],
            ["Sonnet", "Fourteen lines of iambic pentameter. The **Shakespearean** pattern is abab cdcd efef gg (three quatrains and a couplet). The **Petrarchan** has an eight-line octave (abbaabba) and a six-line sestet (for example cdecde); a turn often comes at the ninth line."],
            ["Blank verse", "Unrhymed iambic pentameter. Shakespeare's plays use it for noble characters and serious moments; it is close to natural English speech."],
            ["Free verse", "No fixed metre or rhyme. Rhythm comes from line breaks, repetition and sound."],
        ]),
        T("**Enjambment** is a sentence running over the end of a line without a pause. It speeds the reading and can suggest urgency or overflowing feeling. A line that ends with a pause is **end-stopped**. A **caesura** is a pause inside a line, often marked by punctuation. It can break the rhythm to show doubt, shock or a change of thought."),
        H("Commenting on effect"),
        T("The Board's examiners wanted two things in the poetry task: correct identification and a comment on effectiveness. Use the formula **Name, quote, effect**: name the feature (for example, trochaic metre), quote the line or a few words, then say what it does to the meaning or mood."),
        MISTAKE("Naming without effect", "Writing 'the poem has an abab rhyme scheme' earns little. Add what it does: 'the alternating rhymes give a steady, ordered feel that matches the speaker's calm acceptance'."),
        MISTAKE("Forcing every line into a perfect pattern", "Poets vary the metre on purpose. If a line does not fit, say where the pattern breaks (for example, an opening stressed syllable or a spondee) and what the break emphasises."),
        QUIZ("A line has twelve syllables in four groups of u u /. What is its metre?", ["Iambic hexameter", "Anapaestic tetrameter", "Dactylic tetrameter", "Trochaic hexameter"], 1,
             "Each foot has three syllables with the stress last (u u /): an anapaest. Four feet make a tetrameter.", hint="Look at the foot first, then count the feet.",
             feedback=["An iamb has only two syllables (u /), so twelve syllables would give six feet, but the groups here are three syllables.", "", "A dactyl starts with the stress (/ u u).", "A trochee has two syllables (/ u)."]),
        QUIZ("Which rhyme scheme belongs to a Shakespearean sonnet?", ["abba abba cdecde", "aabb ccdd eeff gg", "abab cdcd efef gg", "abcb defe ghih jj"], 2,
             "The Shakespearean sonnet has three quatrains (abab cdcd efef) and a final couplet (gg).", hint="Three quatrains and a couplet.",
             feedback=["That is the Petrarchan octave and sestet pattern.", "That is a run of couplets, not a sonnet.", "", "That pattern does not match any standard sonnet."]),
        PQ("Scan the line: SLOW-ly the RI-ver comes DOWN to the SEA. (a) Name the foot. (b) Say why the last foot differs. (c) Comment on the effect.",
           "(a) The line has three dactyls (/ u u): SLOW-ly-the, RI-ver-comes, DOWN-to-the, and ends in a single stressed syllable SEA. (b) The last foot is shortened to one stressed syllable (catalectic: the line stops one or two syllables short of a full foot), which gives a firm ending. (c) The dactylic flow suggests the smooth movement of the river, and the single strong syllable at the end sounds like arrival at the sea."),
        PQ("Explain the difference between enjambment and caesura, and say what each can suggest.",
           "Enjambment is a sentence or phrase continuing over the end of one line into the next without a pause, which speeds up the reading and can suggest urgency, flow or overflowing feeling. Caesura is a pause within a line, often marked by punctuation, which breaks the rhythm and can suggest hesitation, shock or a change of thought."),
        PQ("A poem has the end words: home, rain, roam, pain. Give its rhyme scheme. Then say what effect a poet may gain from this scheme.",
           "The scheme is abab (home-roam rhyme, rain-pain rhyme). Alternating rhyme gives a steady, interlocking pattern; it can suggest balance and order, or, if the content is troubled, a tension between orderly form and disturbed feeling."),
        SUM("Scansion marks stressed and unstressed syllables and groups them into feet: iamb (u /), trochee (/ u), anapaest (u u /) and dactyl (/ u u).",
            "Line length names the number of feet: pentameter has five, tetrameter four; iambic pentameter has ten syllables.",
            "Rhyme scheme is shown by letters: sonnets, quatrains, couplets and ballad stanzas have standard patterns; blank verse is unrhymed iambic pentameter.",
            "Enjambment, caesura and variations in the metre have effects that you must explain.",
            "Always follow the formula: name the feature, quote it, explain the effect."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3. Narrative theory
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_THEORY, topic="Narrative theory",
    title="Narrative theory: story, plot, narrator and point of view",
    slug="lil1-narrative-theory",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Narrative theory gives names to how a story is told: story versus plot, kinds of narrator, focalization, order and time, plot shapes, oral prose forms, and how to work out a novelist's purpose.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish story from plot and explain why order and selection matter",
        "Identify types of narrator and point of view, including the unreliable narrator, and say what each effect is",
        "Describe plot shapes (Freytag's pyramid, Todorov's equilibrium) and flashback and other time techniques",
        "Name the main oral prose forms and list clues that reveal a novelist's purpose",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Story and plot", "Narrator", "Point of view", "Focalization", "Unreliable narrator", "Flashback", "Freytag's pyramid", "Oral prose forms"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Two people can tell you about the same bus journey from Douala to Yaounde. One starts with the breakdown at Edea and the other starts with the argument at the motor park, and each makes you feel different things. The events are the same but the **telling** is different. Narrative theory is the study of that difference."),
        T("The Board's report lists 'clues for identifying a novelist's purpose' and 'types of oral prose forms' among the notions tested in the Paper 1 multiple-choice questions, and it asks for narrative technique in essays on prose. This lesson gives you the vocabulary and uses a passage written for practice."),
        H("Story and plot"),
        D("The **story** is the sequence of events in the order they happen in time. The **plot** is the way the writer arranges those events: what is selected, the order of telling, and the links of cause and effect. (The English novelist E. M. Forster is often quoted for the idea that plot stresses causes: 'the queen died of grief' is plot, while 'the king died, then the queen died' is only story.)", title="Story and plot"),
        T("Russian Formalist critics used the terms *fabula* (the raw events) and *syuzhet* (their artistic arrangement) for a similar split. (Check your textbook: critics use these labels slightly differently.) The key idea for you is that **the order of telling is a choice**, and choices have effects."),
        IMG("allil1-story-plot.svg", alt="Two rows of event boxes: the story in time order A B C D, and the plot told in the order C A D B with arrows showing a flashback to A and a jump to D.", caption="Figure 1. The same four events as story (time order) and as plot (the order of telling)."),
        H("Time in narrative"),
        T("A **flashback** (analepsis) tells an earlier event after the main narrative has moved on. It can explain motives or build sympathy. A **flash-forward** (prolepsis) gives a glimpse of the future, creating suspense or dread. The critic Gerard Genette (check your textbook for his terms) also noted that a narrator may **summarise** years in a sentence or **dwell** for a page on one moment; this change of speed tells us what the writer thinks is important."),
        H("Who speaks and who sees"),
        KEY("Types of narrator", "Ask two questions: who is telling, and how much does the narrator know?", cards=[
            ["First-person", "A character says 'I'. We get an intimate voice but see only what this person knows or admits."],
            ["Third-person omniscient", "A voice outside the story that knows what every character thinks and can comment on events."],
            ["Third-person limited", "The narrator stays close to one character, using that character's thoughts and sight, but says 'he' or 'she'."],
            ["Unreliable narrator", "A narrator whose account cannot be fully trusted because of bias, ignorance, self-deception or lies. The term comes from the critic Wayne Booth. The reader must read between the lines."],
        ]),
        D("**Focalization** (Genette's word) is the question of **who sees** in the narration, which may differ from who speaks. A third-person narrator may tell the story through the eyes of a child. **Point of view** is the position from which the story is told and is often used with the same meaning.", title="Focalization"),
        EX("Worked example 1: reading a narrator", intro="Read this invented passage. 'Mama Ngo told me everyone in the quarter loved me. I did not believe her, but I smiled, because at twelve I knew a smile paid for the evening meal. That night the neighbour left a calabash of cocoyam at the door, and I thought: so it is true, or at least they are afraid of Mama.' Identify the narrator, the point of view and the effect.", steps=[
            S("Narrator", text="First person ('told me', 'I thought'): a child, looking back or telling the present, not given."),
            S("Knowledge", text="The narrator sees only what a twelve-year-old would see. The reader may guess more than the child does: the gift could be kindness or fear."),
            S("Reliability", text="The child is honest but limited. The doubt in the last line ('or at least they are afraid') shows the reader the child's lack of certainty."),
            S("Effect", text="The innocent voice makes the reader judge the community for themselves, which gives gentle irony."),
        ]),
        H("Plot shapes"),
        D("**Freytag's pyramid** (a standard pattern for drama and fiction) has five parts: exposition (setting and situation), rising action (complications), climax (the turning point), falling action, and denouement or resolution (the tying up of loose ends).", title="Freytag's pyramid"),
        T("The linguist-critic Tzvetan Todorov described a story as moving from a stable **equilibrium** to a **disruption**, then to recognition of the disruption, an attempt to repair it, and a new equilibrium. Vladimir Propp, studying Russian folktales, found a repeated set of actions he called functions (about thirty-one) and a small set of character roles: hero, villain, helper, donor and others. These ideas help in analysing both novels and oral tales. (Check your textbook for details.)"),
        H("Oral prose forms"),
        T("African literature, and the literature of Cameroon in many languages, draws heavily on spoken storytelling. Learn the main prose forms. A **myth** explains origins or the work of gods and spirits. A **legend** tells of a heroic person or a founding event, mixing history and wonder. A **folktale** is a story of ordinary or animal characters told for entertainment and teaching, and often includes a trickster. A **fable** is a short story, usually with animals, that ends with an explicit moral. Some textbooks add **proverbs** and **riddles** as short oral forms (check your textbook for how your teacher groups them). Oral tales often use direct address to the audience, song, repetition and call and response."),
        H("Finding a novelist's purpose"),
        T("The report lists clues for identifying a novelist's purpose. You can work them out from the text. Look at the title; the opening and closing pages; who the narrator sympathises with and who is mocked; how characters are rewarded or punished; repeated images and symbols; and the narrator's direct comments. A purpose is a statement such as 'to show that greed destroys community', not a plot summary."),
        EX("Worked example 2: purpose in a short outline", intro="An invented novella: A trader cheats customers and grows rich. His wife pleads with him to stop. He ignores her and, at the end, his goods burn in the market fire and neighbours turn away. The narrator describes the fire in plain, unmoved words. State the purpose and support it.", steps=[
            S("Purpose", text="To warn that wealth built on cheating cannot last and to show the value of community."),
            S("Clue: reward and punishment", text="The trader's loss at the end is a kind of poetic justice."),
            S("Clue: sympathy", text="The wife is given reasonable, caring words, so the narrator's sympathy lies with her."),
            S("Clue: tone", text="The plain, unmoved description of the fire suggests judgement without cruelty: the writer lets events speak."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying 'the narrator is the author'", "The narrator is a voice created by the writer. A narrator can be foolish, cruel or mistaken while the writer is not. Say 'the narrator' when you mean the voice in the text."),
        MISTAKE("Retelling the plot to show the purpose", "Examiners complain of narration. Make a claim about the purpose, then support it with two or three clues, each with an example."),
        QUIZ("A writer tells a story that happened over ten years, but begins with the final year and then goes back. Which technique is used?", ["Foreshadowing only", "Flashback (analepsis)", "Soliloquy", "Stream of consciousness"], 1,
             "The narrative jumps back in time to earlier events: a flashback.", hint="Is the telling in time order?",
             feedback=["Foreshadowing hints at events to come; it does not change the order of the whole telling.", "", "A soliloquy is a speech in drama in which a character speaks thoughts aloud.", "Stream of consciousness reproduces a character's flow of thought; it does not by itself reorder time."]),
        QUIZ("Which is a typical feature of an oral folktale?", ["A long description of a single room", "A trickster character and an audience taking part", "A detached narrator who never uses direct address", "Chapters of internal monologue"], 1,
             "Oral tales are told live and often involve a trickster, song and responses from the audience.", hint="Think of a story told in the evening to a group.",
             feedback=["Oral tales move quickly and avoid long description.", "", "Oral narrators usually speak to the audience directly.", "Interior monologue belongs to modern written fiction."]),
        PQ("Distinguish between story and plot, using a short example of your own.",
           "The story is the events in the order they happen in time; the plot is how the writer selects, orders and links them. Example: a story could be 'a boy loses his father, leaves the village and later returns'; a plot might begin with the return, then flash back to the death, so the reader first asks why he returns and learns the reason later. The plot creates suspense and meaning that the plain story does not."),
        PQ("Read the passage in the lesson (Mama Ngo). Explain how the narrator's age affects the way the reader understands the neighbour's gift.",
           "The twelve-year-old narrator sees the gift as proof of love or of fear but cannot judge which. The reader, who is older, can see that community gifts may be kindness, politics or obligation. The gap between what the child understands and what the reader suspects creates irony and makes the reader evaluate the neighbours themselves."),
        PQ("List four clues a reader can use to identify a novelist's purpose, and illustrate two with an imaginary novel.",
           "Four clues: the title; the opening and ending; who the narrator sympathises with or mocks; how characters are rewarded or punished; repeated images or symbols. Illustration: in an imaginary novel 'The Broken Bridge', the title suggests division; if the ending shows the villagers rebuilding it together, the purpose is to show unity overcoming division."),
        SUM("Story is events in time order; plot is the writer's arrangement, linked by cause and effect.",
            "Narrators may be first-person, omniscient, limited or unreliable; focalization asks who sees.",
            "Flashback and flash-forward change the order of telling and create sympathy, suspense or dread.",
            "Plot shapes include Freytag's pyramid and Todorov's equilibrium; oral prose forms include myth, legend, folktale and fable.",
            "A novelist's purpose is found from clues in the text and expressed as a claim, not a summary."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4. Critical approaches
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_THEORY, topic="Critical approaches",
    title="Critical approaches: five ways of reading a text",
    slug="lil1-critical-approaches",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Formalist, Marxist, Feminist, Post-Colonial and Psychoanalytic criticism ask different questions of the same text; this lesson shows each lens, its key names, its limits and how to use it in a balanced essay.",
    objectives=[
        "Say why literary criticism exists and what a critical approach is",
        "State the central question and key thinkers of each of the five approaches",
        "Apply each approach to the same short passage and compare what each finds",
        "Evaluate the strengths and limits of an approach instead of applying it mechanically",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Literary criticism", "Formalist", "Marxist", "Feminist", "Post-Colonial", "Psychoanalytic", "Close reading"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A mango seller, a customs officer and a botanist will each look at the same mango tree and notice different things. A critic does the same with a text. A **critical approach** is a way of reading that has its own questions. The Board's report lists 'the raison d'etre of literary criticism' among the notions tested in Paper 1, so you need to say what criticism is for."),
        D("**Literary criticism** is the careful study, interpretation and evaluation of literature. Its purposes include explaining how a text works, judging its quality, revealing meanings that are not obvious, and connecting a text to its society and to readers' lives.", title="Literary criticism"),
        T("The approaches below belong to modern criticism. Each was developed by particular thinkers, and each asks a different main question. No approach is 'the right one'. Strong essays pick the approach that fits the question and say what it cannot explain."),
        IMG("allil1-critical-lenses.svg", alt="Five boxes, one for each approach, each with its central question: Formalist asks how it is made; Marxist asks about class and power; Feminist asks about gender; Post-Colonial asks about empire and culture; Psychoanalytic asks about the mind.", caption="Figure 1. Five approaches and the question each asks."),
        H("Formalist criticism"),
        T("**Formalism** (including the Russian Formalists and the later New Critics) studies the text itself: its structure, language, imagery, irony and sound. The Russian Formalist Viktor Shklovsky is known for 'defamiliarisation' (art makes the familiar strange so that we see it again). The New Critics, in the 20th century, taught **close reading** and held that meaning lies in the words on the page, not in the author's life. The strength is detailed attention to technique. The limit is that it can ignore history, politics and the reader's life."),
        H("Marxist criticism"),
        T("**Marxist criticism** follows ideas of Karl Marx, who argued that the way a society organises work and wealth (its economic base) shapes its laws, ideas and culture. The critic asks: what class does the text speak for, whose labour and power appear, and does the text support or challenge the existing order? Later thinkers such as Raymond Williams and Terry Eagleton developed this approach. Its strength is linking texts to class and power. Its limit is that it can reduce a work to economics and miss other themes."),
        H("Feminist criticism"),
        T("**Feminist criticism** asks how a text presents women and men, who has voice and power, and how gender roles shape the plot. Simone de Beauvoir (that one is not born but becomes a woman) and Virginia Woolf (women need money and a room of their own to write) are important voices. Elaine Showalter urged the study of women writers as a tradition, and Sandra Gilbert and Susan Gubar studied how women writers worked within a male literary world. Its strength is revealing hidden assumptions about gender. Its limit is that gender alone may not explain a character, and it needs care when applied to texts from earlier periods."),
        H("Post-Colonial criticism"),
        T("**Post-Colonial criticism** studies literature of and about places shaped by empire, and asks about power, language, identity and resistance. Key voices include Edward Said (who studied how the West portrayed the East), Frantz Fanon (on the psychology of colonial rule), Chinua Achebe (who criticised European portrayals of Africa and argued for African voices) and Ngugi wa Thiong'o (who argued for writing in African languages in *Decolonising the Mind*). Homi Bhabha introduced the idea of hybridity, the mixing of cultures. Its strength is giving a voice to experiences that older criticism ignored. Its limit is that it can overlook the differences between societies."),
        H("Psychoanalytic criticism"),
        T("**Psychoanalytic criticism** uses theories of the mind. Sigmund Freud proposed that hidden desires and fears (the unconscious) shape behaviour, and the critic reads characters' dreams, slips and conflicts for these hidden motives. Carl Jung spoke of archetypes shared across cultures, and Jacques Lacan connected the unconscious to language. A well-known example is the reading of Hamlet in which his delay arises from an unconscious conflict (associated with Ernest Jones, who built on Freud). Its strength is exploring motive and symbol. Its limit is that it treats fictional people as patients and can read sexual meanings into everything."),
        EX("Worked example 1: one passage, three lenses", intro="Invented passage: 'The overseer's whistle had sounded long ago, but Ndalla was still bent between the rows of cocoa, counting what she had picked. Her brother, who could read, had gone to town and sent back a letter in the master's language. She folded it, unread, into her wrapper.' Choose three approaches and say what each finds.", steps=[
            S("Marxist", text="The passage shows labour, an overseer and a master: a class structure. The whistle controls Ndalla's time, so her work serves another person's profit."),
            S("Feminist", text="Ndalla, a woman, works in the fields while her brother, who can read, escapes to town. The text may show unequal access to education by gender."),
            S("Post-Colonial", text="The letter in 'the master's language' suggests that a colonial language is a tool of power, and the folded unread letter shows a distance from it."),
            S("Compare", text="The lenses overlap, since all notice power and access. A Formalist reading would instead study the short sentences and the image of the unread letter as a symbol."),
        ]),
        H("Using approaches in an essay"),
        T("Do not write about a text only to prove a theory. Use the approach to ask a question of the text, find evidence, and then judge whether the approach is helpful. Name the approach once, then argue with evidence. For example: 'A Marxist reading of *Coriolanus* focuses on the struggle between patricians and plebeians over grain, but it does not explain the hero's bond with his mother, which a psychoanalytic reading handles better.'"),
        EX("Worked example 2: evaluating an approach", intro="Question (written for practice): 'How far does a feminist approach help us understand a play you have studied?' Plan an answer.", steps=[
            S("Explain the approach", text="State what feminist criticism asks: who has power, how women are shown, whether gender roles drive the plot."),
            S("Apply with evidence", text="Take two or three women characters and say what they do, say and are said to be, with a scene for each."),
            S("Show insight", text="What the approach helps us see: for example, a woman's influence over a public decision made behind the scenes."),
            S("Evaluate", text="What it cannot explain: other forces such as class, politics or the male characters' own flaws. Judge 'how far': useful but not complete."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Applying a label without reading closely", "Writing 'this is a Marxist play' is not analysis. Quote or refer to a moment, then show what the approach makes visible."),
        MISTAKE("Treating one approach as the whole truth", "Examiners reward balance. Say what the approach explains and where another approach explains more."),
        QUIZ("Which approach asks, above all, how a text is made: its structure, language and imagery?", ["Marxist", "Psychoanalytic", "Formalist", "Post-Colonial"], 2,
             "Formalism focuses on the text's own technique and form.", hint="Which approach says meaning lies in the words on the page?",
             feedback=["Marxism asks about class and power.", "Psychoanalytic criticism asks about the unconscious mind.", "", "Post-Colonial criticism asks about empire, culture and identity."]),
        QUIZ("Which pair is matched correctly?", ["Fanon: psychoanalytic reading of Hamlet's delay", "Showalter: study of women writers as a tradition", "Shklovsky: class struggle in fiction", "Freud: hybridity of cultures"], 1,
             "Elaine Showalter urged that women's writing be studied as a literary tradition.", hint="Match each name to its field first.",
             feedback=["Fanon belongs to Post-Colonial thought; the Hamlet reading is associated with Ernest Jones, building on Freud.", "", "Shklovsky is a Russian Formalist (defamiliarisation).", "Hybridity is Homi Bhabha's idea; Freud is linked to the unconscious."]),
        PQ("Explain what is meant by 'the purpose of literary criticism' in two or three sentences.",
           "Literary criticism interprets and evaluates literature. It helps readers see how a text works through language and structure, judge its quality, understand its meanings in its social setting, and connect it to their own lives. It also keeps discussion of literature rigorous by requiring reasons and evidence."),
        PQ("Choose two approaches and say what each asks of a text about a village chief who wrongly punishes an innocent man.",
           "Marxist: the chief holds power over the villagers; whose interests does the punishment serve, and does the text show the unfairness of rule by one person over others? Psychoanalytic: what hidden fear or pride drives the chief's quick decision, and what does his guilt after learning the truth reveal about his mind? (Other pairs are acceptable if they match the approach's central question.)"),
        PQ("Give one strength and one limit of Post-Colonial criticism.",
           "Strength: it recovers the voices and experiences of formerly colonised peoples and shows how language and power are linked. Limit: it can treat very different societies as one, and may concentrate on the colonial relationship even when a text is about other concerns."),
        SUM("Literary criticism interprets and evaluates texts; an approach is a lens with its own central question.",
            "Formalist: how is it made? Marxist: class and power. Feminist: gender. Post-Colonial: empire, language, identity. Psychoanalytic: the unconscious.",
            "Key names include Shklovsky and the New Critics, Marx and Eagleton, de Beauvoir and Showalter, Said, Fanon, Achebe and Ngugi, and Freud and Jung.",
            "Apply an approach with evidence and say what it explains and where it falls short.",
            "Strong essays choose the lens that fits the question and compare it with another."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5. Reading Shakespeare's plays
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_SHAKE, topic="Reading Shakespeare's plays",
    title="Reading Shakespeare: the theatre, the language and the extract",
    slug="lil1-reading-shakespeare",
    minutes=25,
    summary="To read a Shakespeare play you need its theatre (the open-air stage and boy actors), its world view, and its language (verse, prose, soliloquy, old words); this lesson trains you to turn a difficult line into modern English and to place an extract.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe Shakespeare's theatre and the Elizabethan world view and say how they shape the plays",
        "Tell verse from prose and explain soliloquy, aside and other stage conventions",
        "Put a difficult line into good modern English prose in direct speech, following the rules",
        "Place an extract: say who is speaking, to whom, where in the plot and why it matters",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Elizabethan theatre", "Chain of Being", "Verse and prose", "Soliloquy", "Aside", "Paraphrase into modern English", "Placing an extract"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Shakespeare wrote more than four hundred years ago for people who stood in an open-air theatre in London. His English is not modern English, and he assumed that his audience knew things about their world that we have to learn. The Board's 2023 report names **Hamlet** (the old syllabus) and **Coriolanus** (the new syllabus) as the Shakespeare plays set that year. It also says that the new play should be taught from Lower Sixth, starting with background such as biography, dramatic forms, and the Elizabethan world view."),
        T(OWNCOPY),
        H("Shakespeare's theatre"),
        D("**Elizabethan theatre** means English public theatre of Shakespeare's time (Queen Elizabeth I reigned 1558 to 1603, and James I came next). Shakespeare (1564 to 1616) worked with the company that built the Globe theatre in London in 1599.", title="Context"),
        KEY("Features of the stage", "These features explain many choices in the plays.", cards=[
            ["Open air and daylight", "Plays were performed in the afternoon in an open roundhouse. There was no electric lighting, so the dialogue tells us when it is night or a storm."],
            ["Thrust stage", "The stage jutted into the audience, who stood around it. Actors were close to them, which suits soliloquies and asides."],
            ["Little scenery", "Setting was created by words and a few props such as a sword, a crown or a chair. Costume gave rank."],
            ["Boy actors", "Women's parts were played by boys, because women did not act on the public stage. Plays in which women disguise themselves as men (such as As You Like It) used this."],
            ["Features of the building", "A trapdoor (for ghosts and graves), a balcony above (for scenes at windows or walls) and a 'heavens' overhead."],
        ]),
        IMG("allil1-globe-stage.svg", alt="A plan of an Elizabethan open-air stage: a platform in the middle with a trapdoor, a back wall with doors, a balcony above, and the audience standing round three sides.", caption="Figure 1. A schematic plan of a public stage (simplified)."),
        H("The Elizabethan world view"),
        T("Many people believed in a divine order called the **Great Chain of Being**: God at the top, then angels, then kings, nobles, ordinary people, animals, plants and stones. Each level had its place. A king was seen as God's deputy, and rebellion against him was a sin against nature. In plays, a breaking of order (a murder of a king, a mob in revolt) is often matched by disorder in nature or in the mind. This world view helps explain the horror of Claudius' crime in Hamlet and the fear of social unrest in Coriolanus. Funeral rites of the time also appear in the plays, including processions, solemn drums and the carrying of the body, and the Board expected candidates to recognise such details in a given extract (check your own copy for the exact moment)."),
        H("Verse, prose and stage conventions"),
        T("Shakespeare mixes **verse** (usually **blank verse**: unrhymed iambic pentameter, see the prosody lesson) with **prose**. Noble characters speaking on serious matters usually speak verse. Prose is used for comic characters, for ordinary talk, for letters, and for madness (Ophelia and Hamlet at times). When a character moves from verse to prose, or the reverse, ask why. Rhyming couplets often close a scene."),
        D("A **soliloquy** is a speech in which a character, alone or as if alone, speaks thoughts aloud to the audience. An **aside** is a short remark to the audience or to another character that others on stage do not hear. A **chorus** is a speaker who comments on the action. A **stage direction** (such as 'exit' or 'enter') says what happens on stage.", title="Conventions"),
        H("Putting lines into modern English"),
        T("The Board's report says candidates are often asked to render italicised words or lines of an extract into good modern English prose, and that most answers were paraphrased, incomplete or in the wrong tense. The rules it describes are: keep to **direct speech** (the speaker's own voice), keep the **same grammar person and tense** and voice, make the new words **fit the context** and replace the original without breaking the sense, and cover **all parts of the meaning**."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a modern English version", intro="Take this invented, Shakespeare-style line (not from any play): 'Thou hast mistook me, sir; I am no thief that steals by night.' Give it in modern English prose.", steps=[
            S("Decode the old forms", text="'Thou hast' is 'you have'. 'Mistook' is 'mistaken'. 'Sir' stays."),
            S("Keep the same speaker and person", text="The speaker says 'I' and addresses one person. Stay in direct speech, first person, present perfect tense."),
            S("Cover all the sense", text="Two ideas: you are wrong about me; I am not a night thief."),
            S("Write it", text="'You have mistaken me, sir; I am not a thief who steals at night.'"),
        ]),
        TIP("Check three things after writing a paraphrase: Is it in direct speech? Is the tense the same as the original? Could my words replace the original words and still fit the sentence? If one answer is no, fix it."),
        H("Placing an extract"),
        T("The Board complains that candidates mix up characters and fail to place extracts. For any extract, answer five questions: **Who** is speaking and to whom? **Where** are we in the play (before or after which event)? **What** has just happened and what will result? **What** is the speaker's mood or purpose? **Why** does this passage matter to the play (theme, character, plot)? Write the answer as a short opening sentence, then use it to answer the specific tasks."),
        EX("Worked example 2: placing an extract", intro="An extract shows Hamlet alone on stage after the actors have left, calling himself a coward for not acting. Place it.", steps=[
            S("Who and to whom", text="Hamlet, alone, speaking to himself and the audience: a soliloquy."),
            S("Where", text="After the visiting players have performed a speech full of passion, and before Hamlet decides to test the king with a play."),
            S("What and result", text="He blames himself for delay, and decides to use a play to see whether Claudius shows guilt (see your copy: this is the 'Mousetrap' plan)."),
            S("Why it matters", text="It shows his intelligence and self-criticism, and begins the plan that leads to the play within the play."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up characters", "Make a list of the main characters with a one-line role for each at the start of your revision. In an essay, name a character in full the first time (for example 'the tribune Brutus') so that the examiner is sure which one you mean."),
        MISTAKE("Paraphrasing in the wrong tense or person", "If the original says 'I have', do not write 'he had'. Keep person, tense and voice and write direct speech."),
        QUIZ("Which feature of Shakespeare's theatre explains why dialogue often tells the audience that it is night?", ["Electric lighting", "Performance in daylight without lighting effects", "Painted scenery", "A closed roof"], 1,
             "Plays were performed in daylight in an open theatre, so words created the setting.", hint="Think about what the audience could actually see.",
             feedback=["Electric lighting did not exist.", "", "Painted scenery was not used; there was little scenery.", "The public theatre was open to the sky."]),
        QUIZ("Which is a soliloquy?", ["A servant's joke to another servant", "A character alone speaking thoughts aloud to the audience", "A letter read aloud by a messenger", "A song by the chorus"], 1,
             "A soliloquy is a speech by a character alone, giving the audience his or her thoughts.", hint="Soli- means alone.",
             feedback=["That is ordinary dialogue.", "", "That is a read-aloud letter, usually prose.", "The chorus comments on events, but is not a soliloquy."]),
        PQ("Explain two ways in which the features of Shakespeare's stage affect the way his plays are written.",
           "(1) The open-air, daylight stage had no lighting or scenery, so the language had to create the place and time (for example, speeches saying that it is night). (2) The thrust stage put actors close to the audience, which suits soliloquies and asides in which a character confides in the audience. Also acceptable: boy actors meant that women's roles (and disguise plots) were written for them; the trapdoor and balcony allowed ghosts and scenes at walls."),
        PQ("Describe the difference between verse and prose in Shakespeare, with a reason a dramatist might switch between them.",
           "Verse (usually unrhymed iambic pentameter) is used for noble characters and serious or emotional speech; prose is used for comic characters, ordinary talk, letters and sometimes madness. A switch can mark a change of mood, a drop in status, or a character's loss of control. For example, a noble speaker who moves into prose may show disorder in the mind."),
        PQ("Give the modern English prose of this invented line, in direct speech: 'I prithee, friend, take heed lest thou fall.'",
           "'I beg you, friend, be careful that you do not fall.' (Acceptable variants: 'Please, friend, take care that you do not fall.') The speaker is still 'I' and the sentence stays in the present tense, as a request to one person."),
        SUM("Shakespeare's public theatre was open-air, daylit, with a thrust stage, little scenery and boy actors, so language made the setting.",
            "The Elizabethan world view (the Great Chain of Being) helps explain why crimes against rulers and social disorder are shown as grave.",
            "Verse is for noble and serious speech, prose for comedy, ordinary talk and madness; know soliloquy, aside and chorus.",
            "A modern English paraphrase must keep direct speech, the same person and tense, fit the context and cover all the meaning.",
            "Place every extract: who, where, what, mood and why it matters."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6. Tragedy in Shakespeare
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_SHAKE, topic="Tragedy in Shakespeare",
    title="Shakespearean tragedy: Hamlet and Coriolanus",
    slug="lil1-shakespeare-tragedy",
    minutes=26,
    summary="Shakespeare's tragedies show a high-ranking hero whose fall comes from character, choices, other people and circumstance; this lesson outlines Hamlet and Coriolanus and shows how to argue about responsibility, pity and loss.",
    objectives=[
        "Outline the main events of Hamlet and Coriolanus in order and name the main characters",
        "Describe features of Shakespearean tragedy and compare them with Aristotle's model",
        "Argue about the causes of a hero's fall, using evidence and several causes at once",
        "Explain why the hero's death is a tragic loss and name stagecraft elements in the death scene",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Shakespearean tragedy", "Revenge tragedy", "Tragic hero", "Hamartia", "Soliloquy", "Tragic loss", "Dramatic irony"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Shakespeare's great tragedies include **Hamlet, Othello, King Lear and Macbeth**, written in the early 1600s, and later plays such as **Coriolanus** (about 1608, check your textbook for exact dates). Hamlet was the old-syllabus text in 2023 and Coriolanus the new one. The Board's report says Coriolanus seems easier for learners because of its linear plot and less philosophical depth than Hamlet. Ask your teacher which of these your school teaches, or whether another tragedy such as Macbeth is used."),
        T(OWNCOPY),
        H("Features of Shakespearean tragedy"),
        KEY("What most Shakespearean tragedies share", "These features echo Aristotle but differ in important ways.", cards=[
            ["A hero of high rank", "A prince, a general or a king whose fall affects a whole state."],
            ["Fall through character and choice", "The hero's own nature, mistakes and decisions play a large part, but so do other people and circumstances."],
            ["Inner struggle", "Soliloquies show the hero's mind and doubts."],
            ["Waste and loss", "At the end a great person is lost, often with many other deaths, and order has to be rebuilt."],
            ["Comic relief and subplots", "Unlike Aristotle's tight single action, Shakespeare mixes serious and comic scenes and parallel plots."],
        ]),
        H("Hamlet in outline"),
        T("Hamlet, Prince of Denmark, learns from his father's ghost that his father was murdered by his uncle Claudius, who has taken the throne and married Hamlet's mother, Gertrude. Hamlet pretends to be mad and tests the claim by staging a play before the court (the play within a play). He misses a chance to kill Claudius at prayer, kills Polonius by mistake, is sent to England, and returns to find that Ophelia has gone mad and died. In the final duel with Laertes, poisoned weapons and wine kill Hamlet, Gertrude, Laertes and Claudius. Fortinbras of Norway takes power, and Horatio is left to tell the story. (Check the order of scenes and details in your copy.)"),
        H("Coriolanus in outline"),
        T("Caius Martius, a proud Roman general, wins a great victory over the Volscian city of Corioles and is given the name Coriolanus. The Roman people, who are hungry and angry about grain, distrust him. His mother Volumnia pushes him to seek the highest office of consul; he must ask the people for their votes, which his pride makes hard. The tribunes Brutus and Sicinius turn the crowd against him, and he is banished from Rome. He then joins his old enemy, the Volscian leader Tullus Aufidius, and leads an army against Rome. His friend Menenius and others plead in vain, but his mother, his wife Virgilia and his young son persuade him to spare the city. Aufidius, who sees this as betrayal, and his supporters kill him. (Check details in your copy.)"),
        IMG("allil1-tragic-arc.svg", alt="A tragic plot line rising to a turning point then falling to a catastrophe, with labels for complication, turning point, catastrophe and catharsis.", caption="Figure 1. The tragic arc, used here to compare Hamlet and Coriolanus."),
        H("Arguing about the hero's fall"),
        T("Examiners reward arguments about **causes**. In Coriolanus, the Board's report notes the contention that Aufidius is not the only person responsible for the hero's death. Other causes to weigh include: the tribunes who manipulate the crowd and provoke his temper; his mother's influence and the family ties that finally move him; and his own anger, impatience and lack of tact towards the people. In Hamlet, the causes of the final catastrophe include Claudius' crime and plotting, Hamlet's delay and rashness, and the corrupt court."),
        D("A **tragic loss** is the sense that a person of great ability or goodness is destroyed, so that the audience feels waste as well as pity. We show it by pointing to the hero's good qualities as well as the death itself.", title="Tragic loss"),
        EX("Worked example 1: arguing responsibility", intro="Question (written for practice): 'Aufidius is not the only person responsible for the death of Coriolanus.' Discuss with reference to the play.", steps=[
            S("Thesis", text="Aufidius strikes the final blow, but the death results from several causes working together."),
            S("The tribunes", text="Brutus and Sicinius provoke the hero into the angry outburst that gets him banished. Give a scene."),
            S("His family", text="His mother pushes him towards political life; later her plea turns him from Rome and so exposes him to Aufidius' anger. Give a scene."),
            S("His own character", text="His pride, quick temper and refusal to flatter the people make him an easy target. Give an example."),
            S("Judge", text="Aufidius is the immediate cause (his jealousy and the conspirators), but the play shows a chain of causes, so the statement is largely true."),
        ]),
        H("Aristotle and Shakespeare compared"),
        T("Apply the earlier terms with care. In Hamlet, the play within the play and the recognition of Claudius' guilt can be seen as turning points; a recognition of a different kind comes for Coriolanus when he sees his mother kneeling and knows what he has done by yielding. For hamartia, you can argue that Coriolanus' fault is pride and inflexibility, or that his error is trusting to force and to his mother's ambition. Say clearly that critics disagree."),
        EX("Worked example 2: why the death is a tragic loss", intro="Explain why the killing of Coriolanus can be seen as a tragic loss (your own wording, not a quotation).", steps=[
            S("His gifts", text="He is the best soldier of Rome and has saved it before: his death wastes real courage and skill."),
            S("His final act", text="He gives up revenge at the plea of his family, even though it costs him his life: this is an act of love and it makes him more admirable."),
            S("Remorse and praise", text="Even his enemies sometimes honour him; mourning shows that the loss is real (use words from your own copy, in quotation marks)."),
            S("Dramatic irony", text="Rome is saved by the same man it banished, so the city's own treatment of him is part of the loss."),
        ]),
        H("Stagecraft in the death scene"),
        T("The Board's report records that most candidates named the sword (or dagger) as the most significant prop in the killing scene and that few explained how to make it look real on a school stage. Think like a producer: a hidden bag of stage blood, a blunt prop sword, quick movements by several actors surrounding the hero, and a sudden silence or drum before the body is lifted. Cheap, safe methods are valued, and cast and costume should be simple."),
        MISTAKE("Blaming one cause only", "'It was only Aufidius' or 'it was only his pride' gives a shallow answer. Weigh two or three causes and decide which is the strongest."),
        MISTAKE("Narrating the plot", "The Board's report says weak answers narrate. Begin each paragraph with a point (for example, 'the tribunes help to cause his fall') and then use events as evidence."),
        QUIZ("Which of these is typical of Shakespearean tragedy but not of Aristotle's model?", ["A hero of high rank", "A fall that arouses pity and fear", "Comic scenes and subplots mixed with serious action", "A reversal of fortune"], 2,
             "Aristotle prefers a single tight action; Shakespeare mixes comedy and subplots.", hint="Which feature is about the structure of the play?",
             feedback=["Aristotle's hero is also of high rank.", "Pity and fear are the heart of Aristotle's idea.", "", "Reversal (peripeteia) is Aristotle's term."]),
        QUIZ("In Coriolanus, who persuades the hero to spare Rome?", ["Aufidius", "His mother Volumnia, with his wife and son", "The tribune Brutus", "The Roman consul alone"], 1,
             "Volumnia, with Virgilia and young Martius, appeals to him and he yields.", hint="Think about his family ties.",
             feedback=["Aufidius is his ally then rival, and kills him afterwards.", "", "Brutus is a tribune who helped to banish him.", "The final appeal comes from his family; check your copy for the order of those who plead."]),
        PQ("Outline three causes of the death of Coriolanus in addition to Aufidius' final act.",
           "(1) The tribunes Brutus and Sicinius, who stir up the crowd and provoke his anger so that he is banished. (2) His family attachments: his mother's influence and her final plea make him spare Rome, which angers Aufidius. (3) His own character: pride, quick temper and lack of tact towards the plebeians. Each point needs a moment from the play as evidence."),
        PQ("To what extent is Hamlet responsible for the deaths in the last scene? Write a plan.",
           "Introduction: state the question and that responsibility is shared. Point 1: Claudius' murder and plotting (the poisoned cup and weapon) set the trap. Point 2: Hamlet's own actions (killing Polonius, the quarrel with Laertes, accepting the duel) make the catastrophe possible. Point 3: the corrupt court and chance (Gertrude drinks from the cup). Evaluation: weigh which cause is greatest. Conclusion: Hamlet is partly responsible, but Claudius' crime is the main source."),
        PQ("Describe two stagecraft choices for a realistic stage killing of a hero in a school hall.",
           "(1) Use a blunt, safe sword and choreograph the blow in slow rehearsal, with the hero's body turned from the audience, and hide a small bag of coloured liquid to show a wound. (2) Use sound and lighting cheaply: a drumbeat for the blow and a sudden silence, with the other actors closing in so that the audience's attention is on the hero's fall."),
        SUM("Shakespearean tragedy shows a hero of rank whose fall comes from character, choices, other people and circumstance, with soliloquies, subplots and comic relief.",
            "Hamlet: the ghost, the play within the play, deaths in the final duel. Coriolanus: victory, hunger in Rome, banishment, joining Aufidius, the plea of his family, death.",
            "Argue causes: Aufidius is the last cause but not the only one; the tribunes, family and the hero's own nature matter.",
            "A tragic loss is shown by the hero's merits as well as his death.",
            "Stagecraft answers need a significant prop and cheap, realistic production methods."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 7. Comedy and history
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_SHAKE, topic="Comedy and history in Shakespeare",
    title="Shakespeare's comedies and history plays",
    slug="lil1-shakespeare-comedy-history",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Shakespeare's comedies end in harmony after confusion, disguise and the green world, while his history plays dramatise rulers and the cost of power; this lesson gives the conventions of each kind and shows how to write about them.",
    objectives=[
        "List the conventions of Shakespearean comedy and apply them to a comedy such as As You Like It or Twelfth Night",
        "Explain how history plays use English history to explore kingship, order and national identity",
        "Explain why Shakespeare's plays blur the categories, with Coriolanus as a Roman play",
        "Write a comparison of comic and tragic technique with evidence",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Comedy", "Disguise and mistaken identity", "Green world", "Fool and wit", "History play", "Chronicle source", "Tudor view of history", "Genre"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("The first collected edition of Shakespeare's plays, the **First Folio** of 1623, sorted them into three groups: **Comedies, Histories and Tragedies**. Your syllabus names two prescribed Shakespeare plays from among tragedy, comedy and history, and the Board's 2023 report names Hamlet and Coriolanus. This lesson prepares you for comedy and history in case your teacher sets one, and shows how the three kinds relate. As always, check with your teacher which plays you study."),
        T(OWNCOPY),
        H("Features of Shakespearean comedy"),
        D("A **comedy** in Shakespeare's sense is a play whose action moves from confusion and threat to a happy ending, usually with reconciliation and marriages. It is not only a play that is funny: it can contain real sadness and danger.", title="Comedy"),
        KEY("Conventions of comedy", "Look for these in any comedy you study.", cards=[
            ["Love plots and marriage", "Young lovers face obstacles, and the play ends with marriages or reunions."],
            ["Disguise and mistaken identity", "Characters hide who they are (often a girl dressed as a boy, played in Shakespeare's theatre by a boy actor) and others misunderstand, causing both humour and truth-telling."],
            ["The green world", "Characters leave an unjust or rigid court or city for a forest or country, where they are free to change. The critic Northrop Frye called this the 'green world'."],
            ["Fools, clowns and wit", "Comic characters make jokes, puns and commentary that often tell truths wiser characters miss."],
            ["Harmony at the end", "Quarrels are settled and order is restored, often in a dance, a feast or a wedding."],
        ]),
        T("In **As You Like It**, Rosalind, banished from the court, escapes to the Forest of Arden with her cousin Celia and the clown Touchstone, dressed as a young man called Ganymede. In the forest she meets Orlando, who loves her, and she tests his love while he does not know who she is. Several couples marry at the end and the banished duke is restored. In **Twelfth Night**, the shipwrecked Viola dresses as the page Cesario in the service of Duke Orsino; Olivia falls in love with 'him', and confusion follows until Viola's twin brother Sebastian appears. The steward Malvolio is tricked and humiliated, which adds a darker note. (Check details in your copy.)"),
        EX("Worked example 1: comic technique", intro="Question (written for practice): 'Show how disguise creates both comedy and meaning in a comedy you have studied.' Plan using As You Like It.", steps=[
            S("Thesis", text="Disguise creates fun through confusion, but it also allows characters to see and say truths they could not say openly."),
            S("Comedy", text="Rosalind, as Ganymede, advises Orlando about love while he does not know who she is, so the audience, who knows, enjoys dramatic irony."),
            S("Meaning", text="Dressed as a man she has freedom of speech and can test Orlando's love honestly. The forest world lets her think about love without court pressure."),
            S("Stagecraft", text="A boy actor played Rosalind disguised as a man: the audience sees a boy playing a girl playing a boy. Mention the effect of that doubling."),
            S("Evaluate", text="Disguise is not only a trick: it explores identity, and it is solved by a revelation that restores harmony."),
        ]),
        H("History plays"),
        D("A **history play** dramatises events from the past of England (or a nation) from an earlier chronicle. Shakespeare's English histories, such as *Richard II, Henry IV, Henry V* and *Richard III*, drew mainly on historians' accounts (the chronicles of Raphael Holinshed and others). He altered events to make a better play.", title="History play"),
        T("The history plays ask questions about kingship: what makes a good ruler, when may a king be removed, and what are the costs of civil war? Many scholars note that these plays reflect the Tudor view of history, in which the wars of the fifteenth century were a punishment that ended when the Tudor family came to the throne. (Check your textbook: scholars debate how far the plays support or question that view.) **Henry V** presents the king before and after the battle of Agincourt in 1415, with a Chorus who asks the audience to imagine the armies on the stage. The play can be read as praise of a heroic king or as an examination of the price of war: your essay should say which you argue and why."),
        EX("Worked example 2: writing about a history play", intro="Question (written for practice): 'A history play is a lesson about rulers.' Plan an answer on Henry V.", steps=[
            S("Thesis", text="The play shows what qualities a ruler needs and also shows the human cost of his success."),
            S("Leadership", text="Henry inspires soldiers before battle and wins; the Chorus presents him as a model king."),
            S("Doubt", text="Scenes with ordinary soldiers and the king in disguise show the cost of war and the burden of a king's decisions."),
            S("Context", text="The play was written for an Elizabethan audience who valued national pride and who knew the old chronicles."),
            S("Judge", text="The play both praises and questions kingship; a good answer weighs these views."),
        ]),
        H("When the categories blur"),
        T("Shakespeare's plays rarely stay in one box. *Coriolanus* is called a tragedy, but it is also a **Roman play**, based on the Roman historian Plutarch as translated into English by Sir Thomas North, and it deals with politics and a state in crisis like a history play. *Hamlet* contains a gravedigger's jokes. *Twelfth Night* includes the humiliation of Malvolio. Mixing serious and comic is a Shakespearean habit, and noticing it is part of a strong critical answer."),
        MISTAKE("Calling a comedy 'a play that makes you laugh'", "A Shakespearean comedy is defined by its shape: confusion followed by a happy resolution, usually with marriage. Define it by structure, not only by laughter."),
        MISTAKE("Treating a history play as history", "Shakespeare shortened, combined and changed events for dramatic effect. Write about the play's version of events, not about the real king's life."),
        QUIZ("Which setting is typical of Shakespeare's 'green world'?", ["A law court", "A forest where normal rules relax", "A battlefield", "A royal throne room"], 1,
             "The green world is a place outside the court where characters can change and find harmony.", hint="Think of the Forest of Arden.",
             feedback=["A court is the world the characters often leave.", "", "A battlefield is typical of history plays and tragedies.", "A throne room belongs to the court."]),
        QUIZ("On what did Shakespeare mainly base his English history plays?", ["Eye-witness diaries", "Chronicles, such as those of Holinshed", "Newspapers of the time", "Parish registers"], 1,
             "Chronicles of English history, such as Holinshed's, were his chief source.", hint="Think of printed histories of the 1500s.",
             feedback=["Eye-witness diaries were not his main source.", "", "Newspapers as we know them did not exist.", "Parish registers record births, marriages and deaths, not stories."]),
        PQ("Define 'comedy' as Shakespeare uses the word, and give two conventions.",
           "A comedy moves from confusion and threat to a happy resolution, normally with reconciliation or marriages. Two conventions: disguise and mistaken identity (for example Viola as Cesario) and the green world (for example the Forest of Arden). Others: love plots, clowns and wit, harmony at the end."),
        PQ("Explain two ways a history play differs from the actual history it is based on.",
           "Shakespeare compresses long periods into a few scenes, merges or invents characters and conversations, and changes the order of events so that the play has a clear shape and moral interest. He also shapes the story to ask questions about kingship, and he writes speeches that no one recorded."),
        PQ("Why is Coriolanus difficult to place as only a tragedy or only a history play?",
           "It follows a tragic hero's fall and death, which makes it a tragedy; but it is based on Roman history (from Plutarch) and examines politics, the conflict between nobles and people and war between states, like a history play. Critics therefore call it a Roman play or a political tragedy."),
        SUM("The First Folio grouped Shakespeare's plays as comedies, histories and tragedies.",
            "Comedy: confusion, love plots, disguise, the green world, fools and wit, and a harmonious ending.",
            "History plays dramatise English history from chronicles such as Holinshed and explore kingship, power and the cost of war.",
            "Shakespeare changes history for drama, so write about the play, not the real events.",
            "The kinds overlap: Coriolanus is a Roman political tragedy, and tragedies include comic relief."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 8. Writing about a Shakespeare play
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M_SHAKE, topic="Writing about a Shakespeare play",
    title="Writing about a Shakespeare play: essays and context questions",
    slug="lil1-writing-shakespeare",
    minutes=26,
    summary="A method for the two kinds of Shakespeare task, the Paper 2 essay and the Paper 3 context question with parts a to e, with a plan, a paragraph model, the Board's reported complaints and a bank of stagecraft ideas.",
    objectives=[
        "Read an essay question for its command word and focus and plan an answer in five minutes",
        "Write an introduction, a point-evidence-explain paragraph and a conclusion",
        "Answer each type of context sub-question (language, background, quotation, character, stagecraft)",
        "Avoid the faults that the Board's report describes",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Command word", "Plan", "Topic sentence", "Illustration", "Context question", "Quotation", "Stagecraft", "Critical perspective"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Two candidates study the same play for a year. One writes a retelling and gets a low mark. The other makes an argument and proves it with moments from the text and gets a high mark. The Board's report says candidates failed to understand the demands of questions, narrated, ignored terms, left out the critical perspective and wrote without enough illustration. This lesson turns that advice into a method."),
        T(OWNCOPY),
        H("The two kinds of task"),
        KEY("Where Shakespeare appears", "From the Board's 2023 report. Check the current format with your teacher.", cards=[
            ["Paper 1 (multiple choice)", "Four questions on each set text, among forty text questions, on background, setting, plot, structure, style, themes, characters, stagecraft and mood."],
            ["Paper 2 (essays)", "Essay questions on the set texts in sections for drama, prose and poetry; candidates choose four questions in all, from different texts."],
            ["Paper 3 (context)", "Compulsory context questions on drama texts, with sub-tasks a to e on an extract; also unseen prose and poetry."],
        ]),
        H("The essay"),
        T("Read the question and mark three things: the **command word** (examine, discuss, to what extent, how far, do you agree, justify), the **focus** (a character, a theme, a technique) and the **limits** (which play, how many examples). The report asks teachers to train students on these tags from early in the year."),
        D("A good essay has a **focused introduction** (answer the question in one or two sentences), a **body** where each paragraph begins with a topic sentence, then gives several illustrations and explains their effect, and a **conclusion** that gives a clear judgement.", title="Structure"),
        T("Illustration means evidence from the text: events, speeches, stage actions or a few exact words. The report says that one sketchy piece of evidence per paragraph earns little, and that fuller paragraphs with more focused illustrations earn more. Add the **critical perspective**: say what the device or event does and how it improves other elements of the play, such as theme, character or tension."),
        IMG("allil1-essay-plan.svg", alt="A flow of five boxes: read the question, plan, introduction, body paragraphs with point evidence effect, conclusion, with suggested minutes.", caption="Figure 1. A suggested use of time for a Shakespeare essay (a KKEVO practice setting, not a Board rule)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a plan with placeholders", intro="Question (written for practice): 'The tribunes are more to blame than Coriolanus for his banishment. Discuss.' (use this model for any play, replacing the details with your own copy).", steps=[
            S("Decode", text="Command word: discuss (weigh both sides). Focus: causes of the banishment. Limit: the tribunes against the hero."),
            S("Thesis", text="The tribunes provoke him, but his own temper and pride make the banishment possible."),
            S("Paragraph 1: tribunes", text="Topic sentence; two or three moments where they manipulate the crowd (scene and what they say or do); effect: they create the trap."),
            S("Paragraph 2: the hero", text="Topic sentence; two moments of his anger and contempt for the people; effect: he supplies the reason they need."),
            S("Paragraph 3: other pressures", text="Family or politics that push him towards public office; effect: a wider cause."),
            S("Conclusion", text="Judge: both are to blame, and the hero's nature is the more decisive. Say why."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: one body paragraph", intro="Write one paragraph for 'Paragraph 2: the hero'. Use [moment] where you must add a real moment from your own copy.", steps=[
            S("Topic sentence", text="Coriolanus' scorn for the common people gives the tribunes the weapon they need."),
            S("Evidence 1", text="In [scene and moment], he [action or words], which shows his contempt."),
            S("Evidence 2", text="Later, when [moment], he again [action or words], which turns the crowd against him."),
            S("Effect", text="These moments show that his pride is stronger than his political sense, and they make his fall seem to grow from his own character."),
            S("Link", text="Thus, although the tribunes plan the trap, he walks into it, so the responsibility is shared."),
        ]),
        H("The context question (parts a to e)"),
        T("The Board's report describes the five kinds of sub-task on an extract. Learn what each wants."),
        KEY("What each sub-task wants", "Based on the Board's 2023 description of Paper 3.", cards=[
            ["Language", "Put chosen words or lines into good modern English prose, in direct speech, same person and tense, and covering all the sense."],
            ["Background and style", "Identify an aspect of the period shown in the extract (for example a funeral custom) and comment on a device such as irony. Use the extract only; do not name features that are not in it."],
            ["Quotation", "Cite exact words in quotation marks that show a point (for example a tragic loss). Copy accurately and give two if two are asked."],
            ["Character or plot", "Argue a reasoned judgement about a character's responsibility or an event's effect, with evidence from the play and not just narration."],
            ["Stagecraft or real-life task", "Name the most significant prop and explain how you would stage the moment as a producer, or complete a practical task such as writing a report. Be inventive, practical and specific."],
        ]),
        TIP("In a quotation sub-task, quote only a short phrase that proves the point, in quotation marks, copied exactly from the extract on the paper. If the question asks for two, give two."),
        EX("Worked example 3: a stagecraft answer", intro="An extract ends with the hero being attacked by several men. Name the most significant prop and explain how you would stage the killing realistically on a small stage.", steps=[
            S("Prop", text="The sword (or dagger). Name one significant prop, as asked, not a list."),
            S("Safety and realism", text="Use a blunt prop sword, rehearsed slowly into a fixed choreography, with the actors blocking the audience's view of the blow."),
            S("Effect tools", text="Hidden bag of coloured liquid, a drumbeat on the blow and a sudden silence, with the hero's collapse and the other actors stepping back."),
            S("Mood", text="Keep the pace quick and the lighting simple; end with the hero's body lifted slowly to show that the loss is tragic."),
        ]),
        H("What the Board says goes wrong"),
        MISTAKE("Not understanding the demand", "Candidates answered a question about setting by discussing symbols in general. Underline the focus and refer to it in every paragraph."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up characters or not placing an extract", "Learn the cast list and place the extract: who is speaking, where in the plot, and what has just happened."),
        MISTAKE("Poor language and handwriting", "The report asks for clear handwriting and acceptable English. Short, clear sentences with linking words (moreover, however, as a result) are better than long confused ones."),
        QUIZ("Which sentence is the best topic sentence for a paragraph about the tribunes' influence?", ["The tribunes are called Brutus and Sicinius.", "The tribunes manipulate the crowd so that Coriolanus is banished.", "In Act 3 there is a scene in Rome.", "Coriolanus is a play by Shakespeare."], 1,
             "A topic sentence makes a point that answers the question.", hint="Which sentence makes a claim?",
             feedback=["This gives information but makes no argument.", "", "This only places a scene and makes no claim.", "This is obvious and does not answer the question."]),
        QUIZ("A question asks for two quotations that show a tragic loss. What should you write?", ["A paragraph from memory", "Two short exact phrases from the extract in quotation marks", "One long speech", "A summary of the plot"], 1,
             "The skill is accurate quotation, in quotation marks, of the number asked.", hint="Think of the 'quotation' sub-task.",
             feedback=["Writing from memory risks error and is not required.", "", "One long speech is not two short quotations.", "A summary is not a quotation."]),
        PQ("Explain what a strong paragraph in a Shakespeare essay contains, and why one example is not enough.",
           "A strong paragraph begins with a topic sentence that makes a point answering the question, gives several illustrations from the text, explains each one and its effect on theme, character or tension, and ends with a short link to the question. One example is not enough because the Board's report says a single sketchy illustration earns only a low mark, while several focused illustrations make a fuller, more convincing paragraph."),
        PQ("Write a plan for: 'Examine the role of Volumnia in the downfall of her son.' (Use your own copy for evidence.)",
           "Introduction: Volumnia shapes her son's ambition and finally his fate. Point 1: she raised him for war and pride, and urges him to seek office (scene and words). Point 2: she pushes him to flatter the people, which goes against his nature (scene). Point 3: her final plea makes him spare Rome and so leads to his death (scene). Evaluation: other causes (tribunes, his pride, Aufidius). Conclusion: she is a major but not the only cause."),
        PQ("Give modern English prose for: 'Pray you, tell me, whither goes he?' in direct speech. Then say what you checked.",
           "'Please tell me, where is he going?' Checked: direct speech retained; the speaker still asks the question to one person; the present tense is retained; the meaning (a polite request and where) is complete."),
        SUM("Read the question: command word, focus and limits; plan before writing.",
            "Write a focused introduction, body paragraphs with topic sentences and several illustrations, and a conclusion with a judgement.",
            "Add the critical perspective: say what each device or event does and how it improves other elements.",
            "For context questions, match the answer to the sub-task: language, background, quotation, character or stagecraft.",
            "Avoid the Board's reported faults: misreading the question, narrating, mixing characters, not placing extracts and unclear English."),
    ],
))
